    "done_when": "tests/test_a_foreign_pid_never_reads_dead.py gains two drain cases built in a temporary crew home: a pointer whose launcher_host is another host with a pid number this host holds, asserting the drain row does not read process_alive true; a foreign pointer with an exit record on disk, asserting the drain row reads process gone through the exit-record route, not abandoned. A third case with a local launcher_host plus a truly absent pid asserts the drain row still reads the process as gone. The first case fails at the base revision (logged) and passes at the head. The negative control, in a scratch copy, restores the bare local probe in the drain view; the first case fails, with the mutation on the red log's first line and an EXIT= line after pytest's output. The gate is tests/test_a_foreign_pid_never_reads_dead.py plus the existing drain test files; both arms are recorded as one JSON object each under baseline_suite and after_suite (revision, command, exit_status, one log_path, failure_ids), each log ending in an EXIT= line, with the head arm adding no failing id. ruff check plus ruff format --check on the changed paths add no finding against the base. The tests neither read nor write the real ~/.config/reckon.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Plan a-stalled-row-says-whether-the-worker-lives, followup f-asrswtwl-drain-view-reads-a-foreign-pid: the drain view classifies a run from the host-gated liveness reading, never from a bare local probe. Read the followup in docs/plans/a-stalled-row-says-whether-the-worker-lives.html. a-foreign-pid-never-reads-dead (merged at 8413ce50) gated list_live, the retained-work interruption, sprint_liveness plus the directory row on a reading proven on this host or backed by an exit record. The drain view near reckon/crew/runs.py:1028 still writes a bare local record_process_alive probe into the record before classifying it, so a run launched on another host whose pid number this host holds reads process_alive true, while one with an exit record on disk can read abandoned. Route the drain view through the same host-gated reading the merged repair introduced, reusing its helper rather than adding a second one.",
